WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.292
All of a sudden, 
all over Europe

00:00:01.292 --> 00:00:03.128
it seems like they're everywhere:

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.754
Cargo bikes.

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:06.381
Delivering mail in Germany...

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:08.299
Amazon packages in the UK.

00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:10.051
Picking up food waste in Paris.

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:12.721
And sperm donations in Copenhagen.

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.764
Yep. That's a custom built

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.434
liquid nitrogen tank shaped like 
a human sperm.

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:18.685
In the US

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.979
whether you order a giant flat screen TV

00:00:20.979 --> 00:00:22.272
or a week's worth of groceries...

00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:25.358
it's likely to show up at your doorstep
via one of these:

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.695
A delivery truck or van with 
a big, powerful engine.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:31.114
They get the job done, 
but they also emit

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.241
lots of carbon, take up 
lots of room 

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:35.160
and kill and injure 
lots of people.

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:36.453
And that's why

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:39.039
cities and companies around the world 
are starting to replace

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.250
some of those big vans with 
electric assist cargo bikes.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.753
But in the US, aside from test runs

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:47.172
from a couple of companies like 
Domino's Pizza

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:49.424
 and small pilot projects 
in a handful of cities

00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:50.759
they haven't really caught on.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.218
So why not?

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:53.970
Why are we in the US

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.556
so wedded to this one tool 
for delivery?

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.933
And is it possible to change course?

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:07.275
Even before the pandemic

00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:09.110
online shopping was already growing

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.237
as a share of total retail sales worldwide.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:12.322
And for many of us...

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:14.699
the pandemic habit hasn't faded away.

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:16.451
Even as stores have reopened.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.036
Take London, for example.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:21.664
Commercial vehicles account for about 
19% of the overall miles

00:01:21.664 --> 00:01:22.665
driven in London.

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:26.336
But they emit 30% of the city's 
transport-related CO2.

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:29.839
That disproportionate impact 
extends beyond pollution.

00:01:30.173 --> 00:01:32.634
They deliver everywhere from 
narrow one-way streets

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:36.262
to super busy intersections causing 
massive traffic problems.

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:38.640
And between 2018 and 2020...

00:01:38.681 --> 00:01:42.769
delivery trucks in London were involved 
in a shocking 41%

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:46.648
of fatal cyclist crashes and 
19% of pedestrian deaths.

00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:49.109
A 2016 study found that 
large trucks 

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.903
made up only 3.6% of vehicles
in New York City

00:01:51.903 --> 00:01:55.448
but are responsible for 32% 
of bicyclist fatalities

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.784
and 12% of pedestrian fatalities.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:01.579
So let's back up and take a bird's eye view 
of the whole delivery process.

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:04.999
You can breakdown urban delivery into two phases.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:09.796
Phase one: My iPhone cover might travel 
from factory to a hub...

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:12.799
before the long freight journey 
via airplane, cargo ship

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.926
or long haul truck to a warehouse

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:16.261
just outside the city where I live.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:17.720
That's when phase two starts.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.347
It's often called the last mile.

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:21.224
Though, the journey is usually 
much longer.

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:23.810
That's when my package is loaded onto 
a delivery truck or van

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.228
which drives into the city

00:02:25.228 --> 00:02:27.188
and eventually stops in front of my house.

00:02:27.355 --> 00:02:28.565
This package might have traveled

00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:31.609
hundreds of miles to get here,
but depending on where you live

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:35.446
this second phase accounts for 
30 to 50% of its carbon footprint.

00:02:35.738 --> 00:02:39.200
And that's partly because the so-called 
last mile is really in efficient.

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:42.370
One study in London found that 
only a small share of vans

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.539
use more than 3/4 of their capacity.

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:46.374
Most use less than half.

00:02:46.624 --> 00:02:48.209
And that's where e-bikes come in.

00:02:50.044 --> 00:02:53.089
Obviously, using bicycles to move goods 
and people through the city 

00:02:53.089 --> 00:02:54.299
is not a new idea.

00:02:54.632 --> 00:02:56.259
UPS actually got its start 

00:02:56.259 --> 00:02:58.011
as a bike delivery service in Seattle...

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:00.889
hauling everything from beer 
and medicine across the city.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.266
Before city leaders remade the streets 

00:03:03.266 --> 00:03:04.976
to prioritize private automobiles

00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:06.561
cargo bikes and trikes like these 

00:03:06.561 --> 00:03:08.938
shared the road with carriages 
and pedestrians.

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:13.234
Today's cargo bikes can come equipped 
with a small electric motor

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.195
that's activated when the pedals are in motion...

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.989
and powered by a rechargeable 
lithium-ion battery.

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:20.825
That extra power is what allows 
electric cargo bikes

00:03:20.825 --> 00:03:22.243
like these ones in London 

00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:24.370
to outcompete delivery vans.

00:03:24.662 --> 00:03:28.333
In a 2021 study that compared 
those cargo bikes to delivery vans...

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:31.169
the bikes obviously had to make 
more trips back and forth

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:32.045
to the pickup point...

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:35.840
but they still traveled roughly the same distance 
in less time than the vans.

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:39.177
In part because they could park anywhere 
and use the bike lanes

00:03:39.177 --> 00:03:40.428
when the van sat in traffic.

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:43.264
They dropped off more packages 
than the vans per hour

00:03:43.264 --> 00:03:45.475
while emitting a fraction of the carbon.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.728
Another study estimated that 
more than half of all urban trips

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:51.981
related to the transport of goods 
could be done by cargo bike.

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:55.902
Those efficiencies have led to massive 
investments in Europe and the UK.

00:03:55.944 --> 00:03:59.948
Thousands of bikes from companies 
like DHL, UPS and FedEx...

00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:02.367
as well as retailers like Amazon and IKEA.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:06.496
But in the US, DHL made headlines 
for doubling its cargo bike fleet

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:09.707
in Miami from four bikes... 
to eight.

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:11.751
New York City's cargo bike pilot 

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:15.880
featured just 350 bikes spread out 
among all these companies.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.591
So what's keeping them from going all in?

00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:21.386
This map of Paris can help us understand why.

00:04:21.636 --> 00:04:23.513
The city has specific rules about

00:04:23.513 --> 00:04:26.557
what kind of vehicles can drive within this area.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:29.269
Delivery trucks are vans made before 2011

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.562
are only allowed within 
the zone on weekends 

00:04:31.562 --> 00:04:33.356
and after 8 PM on weekdays.

00:04:33.356 --> 00:04:35.525
Those who break the rules 
are slapped with a fine.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:40.071
By 2030, no diesel or gas powered vehicles 
will be allowed in the zone.

00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:42.532
These low emission zones are all over Europe.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:43.825
Brussels. Lisbon.

00:04:43.825 --> 00:04:44.993
London. Amsterdam.

00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:46.452
Berlin. Vienna. Rome.

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:48.037
Oslo. Prague. Budapest.

00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:50.623
So for a freight operator 
doing business in Europe

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.876
it makes sense to make the switch 
from vans to cargo bikes...

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:56.629
especially since a lot of these cities also offer

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:59.048
generous subsidies to businesses 
making the switch.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:02.010
And they've invested millions 
in infrastructure

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.013
like protected bike lanes to help 
keep cyclists safe.

00:05:05.179 --> 00:05:06.889
As of fall 2023

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:09.976
the only zero-emission zone 
in the United States 

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:13.730
was a pilot project in Santa Monica 
that actually ended in 2022.

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:17.191
It was one square mile and 
it was voluntary.

00:05:17.191 --> 00:05:19.819
As in, no fines for trucks 
that broke the rules.

00:05:20.111 --> 00:05:21.946
There's also the fact that interstate highways

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:23.906
run right through a lot of 
American cities...

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:25.241
unlike in Europe.

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:27.618
And for freight companies looking 
to move goods quickly...

00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:30.371
vans on the highway seems like 
the obvious answer.

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:33.624
Of course, switching those vans 
from gas to electric

00:05:33.624 --> 00:05:35.960
would definitely help reduce CO2 emissions.

00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:38.671
But electric trucks and vans aren't any safer

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.299
for the pedestrians and cyclists 
trying to share the road.

00:05:41.632 --> 00:05:43.718
Between 2010 and 2021...

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:46.846
pedestrian deaths in the US rose 54%. 

00:05:47.055 --> 00:05:49.223
Among cyclists: 55%. 

00:05:49.432 --> 00:05:52.185
And experts blame a deadly combination
of high speeds

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.394
and bigger vehicles...

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:56.481
which are more likely to cause fatal 
head and neck injuries.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.526
Without big changes like 
urban highway removals

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.820
zero emission zones and subsidies

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.489
US cities will have a hard time

00:06:04.489 --> 00:06:06.282
getting companies to make the switch.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:10.453
And we will miss out 
on the possibility of cleaner air...

00:06:10.661 --> 00:06:13.331
safer streets and a more livable planet.

